A||||2||Phaeton||4sp auto swap in 88GT  need info||||5||
Z||000000||Phaeton||12-04-1999||08:07 PM||||I finished my all aluminum 3.2 and want to put it in my 88GT this summer with a 4sp auto w/lockin torque converter.  I'm going to use a trans from a beretta or cavalier.  Found this forum before I got around to doing any major research.  Has anyone done this?  I don't mind fabricating.  Mostly I wonder about rigidity and/or any handling problems encountered.  The car is dialed in very well but my driving habits kill the clutch in under 20k (my third clutch just slipped).  No substitute for hands on, if you've done the trial and error I would just love to benifit from your experience.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tim||12-04-1999||09:54 PM||||I have researched this, I found that the TH-440 is about 35 pounds heavier then the TH-125.  Has different final ratios for different applications(WAGONS).  Mid 80's has same mounts as Fiero.  Axles from a Pontiac 6000 with small wheel bolt pattern will bolt-on.  The Fiero shifter has the  detents in it for four forward.  That is the easy stuff, problems include TCC has two lock-ups, 125 has two wires and 440 has three wires.  I saw a diagram on the 'net for converting the TCC to lock-up in third and fourth with only two wires.  I can't remember where.  You will need to construct a shift cable bracket and shift lever to use the Fiero cable.  You may need to re-wire the neutral safety switches.  I may do this as a furture project.  Be warned that the 440 is weaker the the 125.  Trans-Go makes kits to correct problems, check with a transmission shop for details.<p>[This message has been edited by Tim (edited 12-04-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Boxdin||12-05-1999||04:55 PM||||While reading the option codes, I could see that my88 formula a/t has a 3.33 final drive, too many rpm for comfy cruising. but the list also shows 3.18, among others. Why cant we get a lower final drive from gm or  aftermarket sources ?||||reg||6
